# systemd-pcrosseparator.service breaks eventlog-based PCR prediction

Status: root cause confirmed (arithmetically verified)
Date: 2026-09-12
Affected host: w-mrw (Arch Linux, systemd 261.3-1-arch, mkinitcpio 42-1)

---

## 1. Symptom

After a routine `pacman -Syyu` on 2026-09-12, the PCR values displayed by the
tpm2-kira pre-unlock measure point (initramfs, before LUKS unlock) no longer
matched the expected/sealed values.

    PCR0 expected (sealed blob / eventlog replay) : ca606bea...
    PCR0 observed at the measure point            : b9f41487...

The confusing part: `tpm2_eventlog /sys/kernel/security/tpm0/binary_bios_measurements`
run from the booted system still produced the *expected* value `ca606bea...`.

## 2. Why the first readings were misleading

`tpm2_eventlog`'s trailing `pcrs:` block is a **replay of the firmware event
log**, not a read of the TPM. Comparing it against a live `tpm2_pcrread` is
comparing a calculation against a measurement. They diverged, and the
divergence was the actual finding — not noise.

## 3. Evidence gathered

### 3.1 Live PCRs vs. firmware-log replay (MEASURED)

| PCR | firmware log replay | live (`tpm2_pcrread`) | differs |
|-----|---------------------|------------------------|---------|
| 0   | `ca606bea...`       | `B9F41487...`          | yes |
| 1   | `f36b663f...`       | `48E413CD...`          | yes |
| 2   | `3d458cfe...`       | `8D22C738...`          | yes |
| 3   | `3d458cfe...`       | `8D22C738...`          | yes |
| 4   | `e20d688a...`       | `7AB51F7E...`          | yes |
| 5   | `5dc591e2...`       | `BE489889...`          | yes |
| 6   | `3d458cfe...`       | `8D22C738...`          | yes |
| 7   | `55ebe40c...`       | `CCCC4514...`          | yes |
| 8   | (untouched)         | `00000000...`          | no  |
| 10  | (untouched)         | `00000000...`          | no  |
| 13  | (no log events)     | `3345A4E7...`          | non-zero |
| 14  | (no log events)     | `3345A4E7...`          | non-zero |

Two observations did the real diagnostic work:

- **PCRs 2, 3 and 6 changed.** Those hold *only* the firmware `EV_SEPARATOR`.
  No OS-level package update can ever change them. So the cause had to be an
  extra `TPM2_PCR_Extend`, not a changed measurement.
- **PCRs 13 and 14 became non-zero and identical.** Both went from all-zero
  through the *same* single extend.

### 3.2 Hypotheses tested and rejected

- **Firmware measured twice** (warm reset mid-boot, capsule update pass).
  Rejected: replaying the event log 1x, 2x and 3x produced none of the live
  values.
- **Suspend/resume re-measurement.** Rejected: `0` suspend events this boot,
  and the wrong values were already present in the initramfs.
- **tpm2-kira extending PCRs itself.** Rejected: the only `tpm2_pcrextend`
  calls in the tree are in `integration_test.go` against swtpm PCR 23.
- **UKI rebuild changing measurements.** Rejected: explains PCR 4/11/12 at
  most, cannot explain PCR 2/3/6/7.

### 3.3 The finding

`journalctl -k` showed a unit running in the initrd that does not run in the
real root:

    systemd[1]: Starting TPM PCR OS Separator...
    systemd[1]: TPM PCR OS Separator skipped, unmet condition check
                ConditionPathExists=/etc/initrd-release

`/usr/lib/systemd/system/systemd-pcrosseparator.service`:

    DefaultDependencies=no
    After=tpm2.target
    Before=sysinit.target shutdown.target initrd-switch-root.target \
           cryptsetup-pre.target cryptsetup.target
    ConditionPathExists=/etc/initrd-release
    ConditionSecurity=measured-os
    FailureAction=reboot-force

    ExecStart=/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-pcrextend --graceful \
            --pcr=0 --pcr=1 --pcr=2 --pcr=3 --pcr=4 --pcr=5 \
            --pcr=6 --pcr=7 --pcr=9 --pcr=12 --pcr=13 --pcr=14 \
            --event-type=os-separator \
            os-separator

Journal for the unit confirms all twelve extends executed in the sha256 bank.

## 4. Root cause (VERIFIED)

`systemd-pcrosseparator.service` extends PCRs **0-7, 9, 12, 13, 14** with

    sha256("os-separator")
    = ff5b9d73dad709633ae76adf444012b57e913a12ed7403c3931145862f35f841

in early userspace, ordered **before `cryptsetup-pre.target`** — i.e. before
the tpm2-kira measure point and before any LUKS unseal.

Verified arithmetically (exact matches):

| PCR   | base                        | `extend(base, sha256("os-separator"))` | live |
|-------|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------|------|
| 2/3/6 | `3d458cfe...` (log replay)  | `8d22c738fcd1730f...`                  | `8D22C738...` MATCH |
| 13/14 | `0000...` (untouched)       | `3345a4e7857aa5ae...`                  | `3345A4E7...` MATCH |

So `b9f41487... == extend(ca606bea..., ff5b9d73...)`.

## 5. How it reached us without a systemd upgrade

systemd 261.3 was already installed; the unit existed on disk beforehand. The
triggering change was **`mkinitcpio 41.1-1 -> 42-1`**, which began pulling
`systemd-pcrosseparator.service` into the generated initramfs. Hence a change
attributed to mkinitcpio altered PCRs 0-7.

**Lesson:** an initramfs generator upgrade can change the set of units that run
in early boot, and therefore change PCR values, without any of the measured
components changing.

## 6. Why systemd introduced it

From the systemd NEWS file shipped on this host:

> A new service unit "systemd-pcrosseparator.service" will now measure a new
> separator measurement during early userspace into PCRs 0-7, 9, 12-14, in
> order to isolate firmware/pre-boot measurements from host measurements. This
> is a safety concept to protect firmware measurements on systems where the
> regular firmware separator measurement is missing. It's also useful in
> environments where a software TPM is used, i.e. where TPM functionality is
> only available starting with the OS, but not before. Note that this new
> measurement has an effect on all indicated PCRs, hence might affect relevant
> TPM policies.

Background: a PCR is append-only, but nothing prevents *later* code from
extending a PCR conceptually owned by an earlier boot stage. Firmware is
supposed to close its phase with `EV_SEPARATOR` (digest `df3f6198...`), which
turns the firmware's final value into a fixed point that later extends can only
move away from. Without it, compromised OS code could keep extending PCR 0 with
chosen values until it reproduces a known-good state and satisfies a policy it
should not. systemd adds its own separator for the cases where that guarantee
is absent — firmware that omits `EV_SEPARATOR`, and vTPM/confidential-computing
setups where no pre-boot stage exists at all.

**PCR 11 is deliberately excluded** from the list, so signed UKI policies
(`systemd-measure`, `systemd-cryptenroll --tpm2-public-key`) are unaffected.

## 7. Impact

### 7.1 On tpm2-kira eventlog-source PCRs (`0e`, `7e`, ...) — STRUCTURAL

The separator is **not** written to `/sys/kernel/security/tpm0/binary_bios_measurements`.
That file is the firmware's TCG log; systemd records its own measurements in
`/run/log/systemd/tpm2-measure.log`.

Therefore any prediction that replays the firmware log is now permanently one
extend short on PCRs 0-7, 9, 12, 13, 14. This does **not** self-heal with a
reseal — the calculation method itself is now wrong.

### 7.2 On register-source sealing / TPM2 LUKS auto-unlock — ONE-TIME

Any policy bound to PCRs 0-7, 9, 12, 13, 14 that was enrolled *before* the
change holds pre-separator values and will fail to unseal once.
Re-enrolling/resealing from the running system captures post-separator values,
which are what the next boot presents. `sha256("os-separator")` is a constant,
not a per-boot value, so it is stable afterwards.

PCR 11-based signed policies: unaffected.

This host is currently **not** using TPM2 auto-unlock (kernel cmdline is bare
`rd.luks.name=...` with no `tpm2-device=auto`; a ~20s gap between the separator
finishing and `systemd-cryptsetup` setting the cipher indicates a typed
passphrase). Verify with:

    systemd-cryptenroll /dev/disk/by-uuid/<uuid>
    cryptsetup luksDump /dev/disk/by-uuid/<uuid> | grep -iA6 tokens

### 7.3 PCR 9 and PCR 11 — two further findings from verify_os_separator.py

Running the full-chain check (firmware log + `/run/log/systemd/tpm2-measure.log`)
on this host reconstructed every PCR and turned up two things the separator
alone did not explain:

- **PCR 9** is *not* explained by replay + one separator. It is
  `replay + os-separator + 4x nvpcr-init:{verity,hardware,cryptsetup,login}`,
  measured by `systemd-tpm2-setup` ("4 NvPCRs initialized"), which runs in the
  real root **after the disk is unlocked**. So PCR 9 is correct at tpm2-kira's
  measure point; it is the *post-boot register* that is polluted.
  An earlier conclusion that "the event log does not describe PCR 9" was WRONG:
  it compared a post-boot register against a measure-point prediction.
- **PCR 11** receives `enter-initrd` from `systemd-pcrphase-initrd.service`
  74 ms before tpm2-kira starts, so the measure point includes it too. This is
  unrelated to the separator and predates it.

Consequence: the live register at reseal time is a valid reference **only** for
PCRs that stop changing at the measure point. PCRs 9, 11 and 15 do not, and
cannot be used to validate a prediction.

## 8. Decision

**Chosen: keep the separator, order tpm2-kira after it, and model the extra
extend in the eventlog/predict calculation.**

`systemd-pcrosseparator.service` is `Type=oneshot` with `RemainAfterExit=yes`,
so `After=` genuinely waits for completion, and once it is done nothing else
extends PCRs 0-7, 9, 12-14 before switch-root.

### Rejected: ordering tpm2-kira *before* the separator

Tempting, because `e` and `p` already target end-of-firmware and would need no
code change, and because it would decouple tpm2-kira from systemd's early-boot
behaviour entirely. Rejected for two reasons:

1. `tpm2-kira run` is a **continuous loop** (`cmd/scan.go`, `RunCommand`) that
   re-evaluates the sealed slots against live PCRs on every iteration, for the
   whole duration of the password prompt. The separator must fire before
   `cryptsetup-pre.target`, i.e. *during* that loop. Sealed to pre-separator
   values, the display would show a valid TOTP code and then flip to a PCR
   mismatch while the user watches. A flapping security indicator is worse
   than a wrong one.
2. `Type=simple` means systemd considers the unit started the moment it forks,
   so `Before=` would provide no real synchronisation anyway.

It is also zero-sum on sources: it fixes `e` but breaks `r`, because reseal
runs from the booted system where the registers are already post-separator.

### Rejected: reseal against register-source PCRs only

Simple, but loses eventlog-based prediction for the affected indices.

## 8a. Implementation notes

- Hardcode the **word**, not the digest: systemd extends into every enabled
  bank, so a `--sha1` seal needs `sha1("os-separator")`.
- Detection must not be "is the unit installed on the host". There are four
  independent ways for the extend not to happen: unit absent from the image,
  `ConditionSecurity=measured-os` false, `--graceful` with no usable TPM, and
  the `efi_measured_os()` runtime skip in `pcrextend.c`.
- Prefer `$BUILDROOT` inspection at initramfs build time (predicts the *next*
  boot) over runtime probing (describes *this* boot). They disagree exactly on
  the transition boot, which is the case that caused this issue.
- A self-validating runtime probe is available and needs no systemd internals:
  for a stable PCR, `live[i] == extend(replay[i], H("os-separator"))` proves
  the separator ran and re-proves the constant. PCRs 2, 3, 6 are ideal probes.
  Anything that matches neither branch must **fail loudly**, not guess.

## 9. Caveats / traps

- `ConditionSecurity=measured-os` — if false, the unit is **skipped** and no
  separator is extended. Rescue images, live USBs, and initramfs images built
  without the unit present pre-separator values, so a post-separator
  enrollment will not unseal there. Always keep a recovery passphrase.
- `--graceful` — exits successfully when no TPM2 device is found, again
  producing no separator.
- `FailureAction=reboot-force` — do not mask this unit casually. Any TPM2
  enrollment made while it was active stops working if it is later disabled.
- The unit only runs in the initrd (`ConditionPathExists=/etc/initrd-release`),
  so the extends happen exactly once per boot.

## 10. Diagnostic commands used

    # live PCR state (ground truth)
    tpm2_pcrread sha256

    # firmware log replay (a calculation, NOT the TPM state)
    tpm2_eventlog /sys/kernel/security/tpm0/binary_bios_measurements

    # systemd's own measurement log (where the separator IS recorded)
    jq . /run/log/systemd/tpm2-measure.log

    # the unit and its effect
    cat /usr/lib/systemd/system/systemd-pcrosseparator.service
    journalctl -b -u systemd-pcrosseparator.service

    # is the unit inside the UKI's initramfs?
    lsinitcpio /boot/EFI/Linux/arch-linux.efi | grep -i pcrosseparator

    # ordering relative to our own unit
    journalctl -b -o short-precise \
      -u systemd-pcrosseparator.service -u tpm2-kira.service

## 10a. Resolution as implemented

- `cmd/measurepoint.go` models the measure point: `os-separator` on PCRs
  0-7, 9, 12, 13, 14 and `enter-initrd` on PCR 11, hashing the **word** per
  bank. `DetectMeasurePointExtends` probes stable PCRs against the live
  register and returns an error naming the PCR when it matches neither
  branch. `--measure-point=auto|on|off` overrides it.
- `cmd/ukipredict.go` computes PCR 11 natively from the unified kernel image:
  per section `H(name + NUL)` then `H(section bytes)`, in systemd-stub's order,
  then the boot phases. Verified against this host's event log: the section
  replay reproduces `7a7a13a7...` exactly, `.osrel`, `.cmdline`, `.uname` and
  `.sbat` content digests match, and the name digest is over the NUL-terminated
  ASCII name (the event log merely *renders* it as UTF-16).
- New `u` PCR source (`11u`, `11u:/path.efi`). The external `p:COMMAND` predict
  source was **removed** — it is no longer needed, and removing it closes
  `docs/pentest2/vuln-0001` (arbitrary command execution from a planted blob,
  since the command string was stored in the blob and executed by `reseal`).
- Blob version 7 records `measure_point_extends` and `measure_point_detection`,
  so `tpm2-kira info` states what was applied, and verification reproduces the
  sealed values rather than re-detecting.
- `tpm2-kira.service` gained `After=systemd-pcrosseparator.service` and
  `After=systemd-pcrphase-initrd.service`. Previously there was **no ordering
  edge at all** between tpm2-kira and these units, so which side of the extends
  the measure point landed on was non-deterministic.
- The mkinitcpio install hook inspects `$BUILDROOT`, prints which measure-point
  units are active, and records the verdict for the post hook to pass to
  `reseal` — this is what makes the transition rebuild correct.
- `verify_os_separator.py` reconstructs the full chain and is the tool that
  found the PCR 9 and PCR 11 behaviour.

## 11. Transferable lessons
- `tpm2_eventlog`'s `pcrs:` block is a **replay**, not a read. Never treat it
  as the TPM's state. Comparing replay against `tpm2_pcrread` is itself the
  most useful diagnostic available.
- **PCRs 2, 3 and 6 are the canary.** They contain only the firmware
  separator, so their value is a machine-independent constant
  (`3d458cfe...` for an empty-then-separator sha256 PCR). If they change,
  something is doing extra extends — it is never a changed measurement.
- Firmware PCRs are **not** frozen after `ExitBootServices`. The OS can and now
  does extend them. "Only firmware touches PCR 0-7" is no longer true.
- A measurement log and the PCRs it purports to describe come from **different
  producers**. Firmware writes `binary_bios_measurements`; systemd writes
  `/run/log/systemd/tpm2-measure.log`. Replaying only one will silently
  under-count.
- Distinguish "the value changed once" (re-enroll fixes it) from "the
  prediction method is now wrong" (code fix required). They look identical at
  the first failed boot.
